$$\begin{align*}
h(x) &= f(g(x)) \\
&= \sqrt{4 - x^2} \\
&= (4 - x^2)^{\frac{1}{2}}
\end{align*}$$
Using the chain rule to find the derivative:
$$\begin{align*}
y' &= \frac{1}{2}(4 - x^2)^{-\frac{1}{2}} \times (-2x) \\
&= \frac{-x}{\sqrt{4 - x^2}}
\end{align*}$$

Let $u=x^2+3$ and $v=\sqrt{x+2}$.
$$\begin{align*}
u' &= 2x \\
v' &= \frac{1}{2\sqrt{x+2}}
\end{align*}$$
Using the quotient rule:
$$\begin{align*}
\frac{d}{dx}\left(\frac{x^2+3}{\sqrt{x+2}}\right) &= \frac{2x\sqrt{x+2}-(x^2+3)\left(\frac{1}{2\sqrt{x+2}}\right)}{x+2} \\
&= \frac{\frac{4x(x+2)}{2\sqrt{x+2}}-\frac{x^2+3}{2\sqrt{x+2}}}{x+2} \\
&= \frac{4x(x+2)-(x^2+3)}{2\sqrt{x+2}(x+2)} \\
&= \frac{4x^2+8x-x^2-3}{2(x+2)^{\frac{3}{2}}} \\
&= \frac{3x^2+8x-3}{2(x+2)^{\frac{3}{2}}}
